1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
One sky. We saw — many ways of seeing
it.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:10,000
China saw a dragon, a Forbidden City and
a white tiger.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Mongolians tied the sky to a golden
stake. An Evenki hunter chases a moose,
night after night.

4
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:23,000
Hawaiʻi, Tonga, Anuta: sailors found
their way home by the stars, with no
instruments.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:28,000
The Blackfoot painted the Big Dipper and
the Pleiades on tipi tops.

6
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Belarusian farmers saw the Big Dipper as
Elijah’s fiery chariot.

7
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:38,000
The Kamilaroi and Euahlayi linked dark
lanes, not just stars.

8
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:44,000
One Big Dipper: a ship to the Bugis, a
fishing net to the Mandar.

9
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
Some saw a palace, some farm tools, some
the way home.

10
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Yet almost every culture did the same:
looked up and wrote its days on the sky.

11
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:08,000
After dark, the Summer Triangle stands
high in the east: Vega, Deneb, Altair.

12
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:15,000
Vega and Altair: the Weaver and the
Cowherd. Their night, Qixi, falls on 8
August.

13
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:19,000
Thank you for looking up with us.

14
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:23,000
The Azure Dragon: why China saw a
dragon.

15
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:26,000
Follow for a new sky every week.

